  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "For a triangle with side a split into segments m and n by a cevian of length d, Stewart's theorem gives b^2*m + c^2*n = a*(d^2 + m*n); the file derives this from two law-of-cosines equations and records the median special case.",
    "plain": "Develops the Stewart theorem extension of the law of cosines. It starts from the cosine-law equations in the two subtriangles cut by a cevian, cancels the angle terms, and specializes the result to the median of a right triangle.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Stewart's theorem is a standard bridge from the law of cosines to cevian and median length formulas.",
      "The formalization isolates the algebraic cancellation that turns two cosine-law equations into a reusable triangle identity.",
      "The right-triangle median check provides a concrete sanity test for the general formula."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "`stewarts_algebraic` records the algebraic rearrangement behind Stewart's theorem.",
      "`stewarts_from_cosines` derives the Stewart identity from the two subtriangle law-of-cosines equations.",
      "`stewarts_theorem` packages the standard b^2*m + c^2*n = a*(d^2 + m*n) form.",
      "`median_length_formula` and `right_triangle_median` specialize the identity to the median formula and the 3-4-5 triangle check."
    ],
    "open": [
      "A fully synthetic Euclidean-geometry version with explicit point and angle objects remains outside this algebraic file."
    ],
    "goal": "Make the Stewart-theorem law-of-cosines extension discoverable from the problem metadata."
  },
